<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rama I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1737">1737</a>–<a href="/years/1809">1809</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Phutthayotfa Chulalok</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> I, known before his accession as Chao Phraya Chakri, was a general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">King Taksin</a> of Thonburi who drove the Burmese from Lan Na and brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerald-buddha">Emerald Buddha</a> from Vientiane in 1779. In 1782 he became king, founding the Chakri dynasty and the new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, the Rattanakosin Kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rama-i.jpg" alt="Rama I" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Rama_I_of_Siam_(Yodfa_Chulalok)_Portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rama I was born Thongduang in 1737, during the reign of King Boromakot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>. His father, Thongdi, was a nobleman of Mon ancestry at the royal court, a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosa-pan">Kosa Pan</a>, who had led King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narai">Narai</a>'s embassy to the French court. His mother, Daoreung, was the eldest daughter of a wealthy Hokkien Chinese magnate. As a young man he entered the court as a royal page to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uthumphon">Uthumphon</a>, spent a time as a monk in 1757, married Nak in 1760 and became deputy governor of Ratchaburi, a frontier town on the western approaches of the kingdom.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> fell to the Burmese in 1767, he joined Phraya Wachiraprakan, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">King Taksin</a>, who founded the Kingdom of Thonburi in 1768. Together with his brother Bunma he became Taksin's most successful general. He rose to Chaophraya Chakri, chief minister for the northern provinces, and commanded armies against the Burmese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> and Lao. In 1774 he went to Lan Na to free it from Burmese rule with the help of Phraya Kawila of Lampang, and in 1778 the three Lao kingdoms of Vientiane, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luang-prabang">Luang Prabang</a> and Champasak fell to Siam. He became the first Somdet Chao Phraya, a rank equal to that of royalty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1781 he was campaigning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> when the rebellion of Phraya Sun deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">Taksin</a>. Returning in 1782, he defeated the rebels, took power and crowned himself on 6 April 1782, founding the Chakri dynasty. Later sources widely report that Taksin was executed, contradicting some earlier ones. Rama I moved the capital to the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chao-phraya-river">Chao Phraya River</a>, for its better position and to start with a clean slate, and named it Rattanakosin, keeping place of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerald-buddha">Emerald Buddha</a>, which he installed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-phra-kaew">Wat Phra Kaew</a> within the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-palace">Grand Palace</a>.</p><p>The Burmese–Siamese War of 1785 to 1786, called the Nine Armies War, was the last major Burmese assault on Siam; King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodawpaya">Bodawpaya</a>'s attack was defeated, and a second attempt the next year was quickly beaten at Tha Din Daeng. He convened a council on the Buddhist canon in 1788, and in 1805 the court compiled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-seals-law">Three Seals Law</a>, which served as a reference for Siam's traditional legal system into the modern reform era.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rama I died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> on 7 September 1809, aged 72, after a short acute illness, and was succeeded by his son Prince Isarasundhorn as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-ii">Rama II</a>. His reign marked a revival of Siamese culture and state organisation after the collapse of the kingdom in 1767, and Siam reasserted itself as a mainland Southeast Asian power with suzerainty over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and the northern Malay states.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rama I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Harsh Burmese rule provoked northern lords, led by Kawila of Lampang and Phraya Chaban, to side with King Taksin of Thonburi, whose forces under <b>Chao Phraya Chakri</b> helped take Chiang Mai in 1775.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>His most successful general, <b>Chao Phraya Chakri</b>, born Thong Duang, returned from a campaign in Cambodia, had Taksin executed and took the throne on 6 April 1782, founding the Chakri dynasty that still reigns in Thailand; he is known as Rama I, or Phra Phutthayotfa Chulalok.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The general Chao Phraya Chakri became king of Siam as <b>Rama I</b> and soon moved the capital across the river to Bangkok.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-6">6 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Rama I</b>, founder of the Chakri dynasty, ordered the palace built when he moved the capital from Thonburi to Bangkok.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-6">6 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His generals, the brothers <b>Chao Phraya Chakri</b> and Surasi, expelled the Burmese from Lan Na and conquered the Kingdom of Vientiane in 1779, bringing back the Emerald Buddha.</blockquote><a href="/state/thonburi-kingdom">Thonburi Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Phra Nakhon Si Ayutthaya, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.36,100.58&amp;year=1783" aria-label="Phra Nakhon Si Ayutthaya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7302 1629 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rama I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7752 1899h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7752 1899h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=14.36,100.58&amp;year=1783">What was here in 1783? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
